\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Rotating the shown reference to match a sample raises that identical sample's score by up to $+9.5$ of 10 points, with mirror-image losses for samples matching the displaced reference and a near-flat placebo: anchoring is causal.} Bars show anchoring effects on the 0--10 score scale at four judge sizes, averaged over two rubric paraphrases; whiskers span the union of the two paraphrases' 95\% bootstrap CIs. Navy: paired same-sample deltas, the score gain of the identical, unchanged sample text when the reference is rotated to match its answer ($n{=}40$ items per cell). Blue: the anchoring margin in the rotation arm, the mean score bonus of valid reference-matching samples over equally valid non-matching ones ($n{=}18$). Orange: the same margin for blind judges, who see no reference, on the same split, mostly null ($n{=}18$). Light blue: the referenced margin in the original-reference arm ($n{=}4$). Light orange: blind judges on the original-arm split ($n{=}4$). Samples matching neither reference stay near-flat across the two runs (placebo), and samples matching only the displaced original reference drop by a roughly symmetric amount (losers); both controls are quantified in Section~\ref {sec:rq2} and Table~\ref {tab:rot}.}}{2}{figure.caption.1}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:anchoring}{{1}{2}{\textbf {Rotating the shown reference to match a sample raises that identical sample's score by up to $+9.5$ of 10 points, with mirror-image losses for samples matching the displaced reference and a near-flat placebo: anchoring is causal.} Bars show anchoring effects on the 0--10 score scale at four judge sizes, averaged over two rubric paraphrases; whiskers span the union of the two paraphrases' 95\% bootstrap CIs. Navy: paired same-sample deltas, the score gain of the identical, unchanged sample text when the reference is rotated to match its answer ($n{=}40$ items per cell). Blue: the anchoring margin in the rotation arm, the mean score bonus of valid reference-matching samples over equally valid non-matching ones ($n{=}18$). Orange: the same margin for blind judges, who see no reference, on the same split, mostly null ($n{=}18$). Light blue: the referenced margin in the original-reference arm ($n{=}4$). Light orange: blind judges on the original-arm split ($n{=}4$). Samples matching neither reference stay near-flat across the two runs (placebo), and samples matching only the displaced original reference drop by a roughly symmetric amount (losers); both controls are quantified in Section~\ref {sec:rq2} and Table~\ref {tab:rot}}{figure.caption.1}{}}
